WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.169
AI governance should be practical enough to support adoption.

2
00:00:04.169 --> 00:00:11.091
If governance is only a slogan or a long document that teams ignore, it will not protect the organization.

3
00:00:11.091 --> 00:00:28.004
A useful program defines approved use cases, data handling rules, model approval criteria, vendor review, access control expectations, logging requirements, evaluation standards, incident response, vulnerability management, and change control.

4
00:00:28.004 --> 00:00:30.285
Governance should be risk-based.

5
00:00:30.285 --> 00:00:42.557
A low-risk internal drafting assistant does not need the same controls as a system that uses regulated data, supports customer-facing decisions, or can trigger production workflows.

6
00:00:42.557 --> 00:00:54.199
The right level of review depends on data sensitivity, user population, tool access, external dependencies, impact of errors, and the ability to detect and correct problems.

7
00:00:54.199 --> 00:01:09.617
Ownership is essential. Teams should know who approves models, who maintains retrieval collections, who reviews tool permissions, who handles incidents, who monitors cost and reliability, and who communicates changes to users.

8
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:17.090
Without ownership, a private AI platform can drift into unmanaged use even when the original design was careful.

9
00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:24.799
Responsible rollout includes pilots, feedback loops, documented limitations, training, and continuous review.

10
00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:29.991
Governance should make safe paths easier, not merely slow down every request.

11
00:01:29.991 --> 00:01:32.980
Sovereign AI is not a one-time installation.

12
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:41.475
It is a managed capability that must be maintained as models, data, vendors, infrastructure, and organizational needs change.

13
00:01:41.475 --> 00:01:44.307
Governance should also produce evidence.

14
00:01:44.307 --> 00:01:55.477
Approval records, model inventories, data-flow notes, evaluation summaries, incident records, and change histories help teams answer reasonable questions later.

15
00:01:55.477 --> 00:02:01.141
What changed, who approved it, what data was involved, and what controls were tested?

16
00:02:01.141 --> 00:02:06.176
Those records make responsible AI operations easier to defend and improve.
